ure/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`),\n41\t  Konflux onboarded, owner RHOAI Serving.\n42\t- **Dashboard** — Active. Code changes: per-project administration control for\n43\t  inference auditing. In platform (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`),\n44\t  Konflux onboarded, owner RHOAI Dashboard.\n45\t- No passive components. No new components (both have architecture context\n46\t  files), so no onboarding chain is generated.\n47\t\n48\t## Step 3 — Investigation Epics\n49\t\n50\tThe strategy declares \"Open Questions: None\" and the audit sink interface is\n51\tstated to be the standard cluster logging forwarder. No conditional ADRs,\n52\tpending reviews, or risks tables are present. The one candidate unknown — the\n53\texact per-project enablement mechanism (CR field vs. dashboard config) — does\n54\tnot change which epics exist or what they do; it is an implementation detail\n55\tfor the serving team, captured as scope/AC on the KServe implementation epic.\n56\t**No Investigation epics.**\n57\t\n58\t## Epic List\n59\t\n60\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n61\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 | Define and publish the common inference audit event schema | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 | Emit inference audit events from KServe with reliable delivery to the cluster audit sink | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 | Per-project inference auditing administration in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n65\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 | Document enabling inference auditing and interpreting audit events | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n66\t\n67\tNotes:\n68\t- E001 is labeled P2 in HLR-4 but is raised to P0 by priority inheritance: it\n69\t  transitively enables HLR-1/HLR-2 (all P0 work) — the strategy itself states\n70\t  HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled.\n71\t- E004 carries `implementation_type: docs-authoring`; its priority is derived\n72\t  from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0) per the\n73\t  docs-authoring priority exception, not from HLR-5's P2 label.\n74\t- Component mapping: E001/E002 → `KServe`, E003 → `Dashboard`, E004 →\n75\t  `Documentation` (all verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`). The\n76\t  documentation epic's team is inferred as RHOAI Serving (owner of the primary\n77\t  audited capability) because the strategy has no Impacted Teams table.\n78\t\n79\t## Dependency DAG\n80\t\n81\t```mermaid\n82\tgraph TD\n83\t    E001[\"E001: Audit event schema (P0)\"] --> E002[\"E002: KServe emission + delivery (P0)\"]\n84\t    E002 --> E003[\"E003: Dashboard per-project toggle (P1)\"]\n85\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004: Documentation (docs-authoring)\"]\n86\t    E002 --> E004\n87\t    E003 --> E004\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\t## DAG Justification\n91\t\n92\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n93\t|------|------|-----------|\n94\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The schema is the format HLR-1's events must conform to; the emission implementation builds against it. The strategy explicitly states HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled. |\n95\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The Dashboard control writes the per-project audit enablement configuration that the serving-side implementation defines; the consuming implementation is serial on the producer of that configuration surface. The exact mechanism (CR field vs. config) is an implementation detail. |\n96\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe the published schema; docs-authoring is blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n97\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe the shipped emission and delivery behavior. |\n98\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe the per-project Dashboard toggle. |\n99\t\n100\tNo konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics are\n101\tgenerated: both target components are in the platform and Konflux-onboarded per\n102\tarchitecture context, and no new component or dependency is introduced.\n103\t\n104\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n105\t\n106\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n107\t|-----|----------|---------|\n108\t| HLR-1 — audit event per inference request (identity, model/version, timestamp, status; no payloads) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n109\t| HLR-2 — at-least-once delivery to cluster audit sink, no loss on restart | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n110\t| HLR-3 — per-project enable/disable from Dashboard | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 |\n111\t| HLR-4 — common audit event schema defined and published | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 |\n112\t| HLR-5 — documentation on enabling auditing and interpreting events | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 |\n113\t\n114\tFull coverage: all 5 HLRs map to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n115\tHLR-1 and HLR-2 are bundled in one epic (same component, same team, same\n116\tlogical change — emission and reliable delivery are one code path in the\n117\tserving stack; splitting them would make two epics build the same surface).\n118\tHLR-4 is not split from HLR-1 by priority label: on a shared surface the P2\n119\tlabel is a sequencing note the strategy itself states (\"HLR-1 cannot ship\n120\tbefore the schema is settled\"), and the schema is a distinct work product with\n121\ta distinct deliverable, so it is its own epic gated ahead of the emission work\n122\t(Rule 13), at inherited P0.\n123\t\n124\t## Health Warnings\n125\t\n126\t- **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n127\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are all absent. Owners were\n128\t  inferred from architecture context; epic ACs were derived from HLR text. A\n129\t  human should verify team assignments before scheduling.\n130\t- **Priority inversion (flagged, mitigated)**: HLR-4 is labeled P2 yet gates all\n131\t  P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2) — the strategy's own note says HLR-1 cannot ship before\n132\t  the schema is settled. Mitigated by priority inheritance: the schema epic is\n133\t  P0. The P2 label on HLR-4 appears to understate its criticality.\n134\t- **Docs priority surprise**: the documentation epic is P0 (derived from\n135\t  strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` via the docs-authoring exception)\n136\t  even though HLR-5 is P2. This is rule-conformant but reviewers should be\n137\t  aware the underlying requirement is P2.\n138\t- **Per-project enablement mechanism unspecified**: the strategy does not say\n139\t  how per-project auditing is configured (CR field, OdhDashboardConfig, etc.).\n140\t  This is an implementation detail for the serving and dashboard teams — it\n141\t  does not change which epics exist — and is captured as scope/AC on the KServe\n142\t  and Dashboard epics rather than as an Investigation.\n143\t- **Tamper-evidence**: the problem statement demands \"tamper-evident\" audit\n144\t  events, but no HLR operationalizes tamper-evidence and the out-of-scope list\n145\t  excludes long-term storage/retention tooling. Delivery to the standard\n146\t  cluster logging forwarder is the strategy's stated answer; if auditors\n147\t  require stronger guarantees, a follow-up strategy is needed.\n148\t\n149\t## Tiered Delivery\n150\t\n151\tNot applicable. There are no external dependencies with uncertain timing: the\n152\taudit sink is stated to be the standard cluster logging forwarder, and both\n153\ttarget components are in-platform and Konflux-onboarded. The DAG is linear\n154\talong the schema → emission → administration path with documentation last.\n155\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b64ac9384f034fea","agent_id":"a4ab00141ce8272de","session_id":"2349ced6-e096-4fba-9cc4-d111ca7d4c3d","uuid":"f9c4ca1a-8a96-461f-ba1b-fcaf3c29b7e5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T18:10:23.791Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d8a5f272997cf4d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type