{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-897ba510604846e4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9010-E001\n3\ttitle: Define and publish the common inference audit event schema\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n5\tcomponent: KServe\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: -1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: -1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 0\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: Medium\n23\tai_implementability_score: 2\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Define and publish the common inference audit event schema\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic defines the structured audit event schema that every inference audit\n34\tevent must conform to, and publishes it as the shared contract for the platform.\n35\tIt is the foundation the inference audit emission capability builds against, and\n36\tit is the format future components (pipelines, workbenches) will adopt so their\n37\taudit events are compatible. The schema exists to satisfy financial-services\n38\tcompliance requirements: auditors need structured, tamper-evident records of who\n39\tinvoked which model endpoint, when, and with what outcome.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Define the common audit event schema, including fields for:\n44\t  - caller identity\n45\t  - model and version\n46\t  - timestamp\n47\t  - response status\n48\t- Explicitly exclude request and response payloads from the schema — audit\n49\t  events record metadata about the request, never the content\n50\t- Publish and version the schema in a location where future platform components\n51\t  (pipelines, workbenches) can find it and emit compatible events without\n52\t  ad-hoc coordination\n53\t- Ensure the schema's structure is deliverable through the cluster audit sink\n54\t  (the standard cluster logging forwarder interface)\n55\t\n56\t## Acceptance Criteria\n57\t\n58\t- The schema defines fields covering caller identity, model/version, timestamp,\n59\t  and response status\n60\t- The schema contains no field for request/response payload content, and the\n61\t  exclusion is documented as a compliance requirement\n62\t- The schema is published and versioned; a component author with no prior\n63\t  context can locate it and construct a conforming event\n64\t- The schema structure has been reviewed for compatibility with ingestion by the\n65\t  standard cluster logging forwarder\n66\t\n67\t## HLR Traceability\n68\t\n69\t- HLR-4 (P2) — a common audit event schema is defined and published so future\n70\t  components emit compatible events. Priority raised to P0 by inheritance: the\n71\t  strategy states the inference audit emission requirement (HLR-1, P0) cannot\n72\t  ship before this schema is settled.\n73\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b64ac9384f034fea","agent_id":"a4ab00141ce8272de","session_id":"2349ced6-e096-4fba-9cc4-d111ca7d4c3d","uuid":"815b29cd-0879-456f-8628-b953e6f1e5b5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T18:10:26.941Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-897ba510604846e4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
