omponents are in the platform and Konflux-onboarded per\n101\tarchitecture context, and no new component or dependency is introduced.\n102\t\n103\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n104\t\n105\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n106\t|-----|----------|---------|\n107\t| HLR-1 — audit event per inference request (identity, model/version, timestamp, status; no payloads) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n108\t| HLR-2 — at-least-once delivery to cluster audit sink, no loss on restart | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n109\t| HLR-3 — per-project enable/disable from Dashboard | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 |\n110\t| HLR-4 — common audit event schema defined and published | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 |\n111\t| HLR-5 — documentation on enabling auditing and interpreting events | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 |\n112\t\n113\tFull coverage: all 5 HLRs map to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n114\tHLR-1 and HLR-2 are bundled in one epic (same component, same team, same\n115\tlogical change — emission and reliable delivery are one code path in the\n116\tserving stack; splitting them would make two epics build the same surface).\n117\tHLR-4 is not split from HLR-1 by priority label: on a shared surface the P2\n118\tlabel is a sequencing note the strategy itself states (\"HLR-1 cannot ship\n119\tbefore the schema is settled\"), and the schema is a distinct work product with\n120\ta distinct deliverable, so it is its own epic gated ahead of the emission work\n121\t(Rule 13), at inherited P0.\n122\t\n123\t## Health Warnings\n124\t\n125\t- **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n126\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are all absent. Owners were\n127\t  inferred from architecture context; epic ACs were derived from HLR text. A\n128\t  human should verify team assignments before scheduling.\n129\t- **Priority inversion (flagged, mitigated)**: HLR-4 is labeled P2 yet gates all\n130\t  P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2) — the strategy's own note says HLR-1 cannot ship before\n131\t  the schema is settled. Mitigated by priority inheritance: the schema epic is\n132\t  P0. The P2 label on HLR-4 appears to understate its criticality.\n133\t- **Docs priority surprise**: the documentation epic is P0 (derived from\n134\t  strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` via the docs-authoring exception)\n135\t  even though HLR-5 is P2. This is rule-conformant but reviewers should be\n136\t  aware the underlying requirement is P2.\n137\t- **Per-project enablement mechanism unspecified**: the strategy does not say\n138\t  how per-project auditing is configured (CR field, OdhDashboardConfig, etc.).\n139\t  This is an implementation detail for the serving and dashboard teams — it\n140\t  does not change which epics exist — and is captured as scope/AC on the KServe\n141\t  and Dashboard epics rather than as an Investigation.\n142\t- **Tamper-evidence**: the problem statement demands \"tamper-evident\" audit\n143\t  events, but no HLR operationalizes tamper-evidence and the out-of-scope list\n144\t  excludes long-term storage/retention tooling. Delivery to the standard\n145\t  cluster logging forwarder is the strategy's stated answer; if auditors\n146\t  require stronger guarantees, a follow-up strategy is needed.\n147\t\n148\t## Tiered Delivery\n149\t\n150\tNot applicable. There are no external dependencies with uncertain timing: the\n151\taudit sink is stated to be the standard cluster logging forwarder, and both\n152\ttarget components are in-platform and Konflux-onboarded. The DAG is linear\n153\talong the schema → emission → administration path with documentation last.\n154\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-81e22835b848ba2e","agent_id":"a3ae4796c76551536","session_id":"2349ced6-e096-4fba-9cc4-d111ca7d4c3d","uuid":"e63de961-45ee-4e73-b174-2c886d9add44","timestamp":"2026-10-22T18:15:04.729Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4be68326d1c09ba","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent